##" HEADLINE ANSWERS (May–July 2026): fly early. Flights scheduled to leave 5–8am arrived late about 10% of the time vs about 38% for 6–9pm departures (at RDU: 5am 7%, 5pm 40%). Fewest late arrivals by airline: Envoy 81.0% on time, Republic 80.6% (but 4.8% cancelled), Alaska 80.1%, Delta 79.5%; Southwest lowest of the big airlines at 69.2%. Tuesday and Saturday were the best days (22% late), Sunday and Monday the worst (27%). This is the best time to FLY (scheduled departure): BTS has no booking dates, so it says nothing about when to buy a ticket. Caveats: one summer season; not adjusted for weather or season; correlation, not cause; "late" = arrived 15+ minutes after schedule, of flights that operated (cancellations are a separate rate). SOURCE: BTS Reporting Carrier On-Time Performance (1987–present), https://www.transtats.bts.gov/Fields.asp?gnoyr_VQ=FGJ, US DOT, public domain; 1,851,282 flights, 1 May–31 July 2026, 354 departure airports, 13 reporting airlines; downloaded 6 October 2026, re-verified 7 October 2026. The view delays_summary -> best_time_to_fly_by_hour gives these answers for any airport and airline. Every scheduled US domestic flight reported to the Bureau of Transportation Statistics (BTS), May–July 2026 (1.85M flights, 92 days): late arrivals (15+ minutes), cancellations and their causes, airports by size, routes, departure hours, and each aircraft followed through its day (tail number) to measure and cost delay chains. STORMS: storm_flights (start here), storm_days, storm_route_pairs compare cancellations on storm days with what the same storm justified (IEM ASOS weather + NOAA Storm Events, 30 busiest airports); views airports_cancelling_more_than_the_storm, airlines_cancelling_more_than_the_storm, regional_vs_mainline_same_route, short_regional_hops_cut_first, cancellations_by_storm_severity, storm_intensity_by_airport. HOW TO ANSWER QUESTIONS: use the fast *_summary sources (pre-summed, same numbers as the slow row-level sources). delays_summary = on-time, cancellations, delay causes; legs_summary = airport comparisons, small airport vs hub, best hour from an airport; routes_summary and route_hours_summary = routes and the best time to fly one; chain_starts_summary = where chains reaching an airport start; chain_slots_summary = the cost of delay chains and which departures to protect. Views named as questions (departures_to_protect_first, morning_departures_to_prioritise, airports_that_start_the_most_costly_chains, delay_chain_cost_by_airline, delay_chain_cost_range, small_airport_vs_hub, best_departure_hour_from_airport, best_time_to_fly_by_hour, airlines_ranked_by_on_time, best_departure_window_by_route, airports_whose_late_starts_reach_here, what_causes_delays, least_reliable_routes, best_day_of_week) answer the common buyer questions and say who acts. Delay-chain cost is always a RANGE: conservative_cost_usd to downstream_cost_usd ($101.3M to $369.9M nationally, May-July 2026). Filter a playbook view with + { where: ... }; to shorten one, use + { order_by: ... limit: N } (a bare + { limit: N } drops the view's ordering). The sources flights, aircraft_legs, delay_chains, chain_slots and airport_sizes are SLOW, for exploring the method only.
## dataset { title="US Flights (BTS On-Time)" }

